.TP
.B vmstat
Print one line of since-boot averages and exit. Useful as a quick
"how's this machine doing" snapshot.
.TP
.B vmstat 1
Sample once per second, forever, until interrupted with Ctrl+C.
.TP
.B vmstat 1 5
Sample once per second for five iterations, then exit.
.TP
.B vmstat 5 \-y
Sample every five seconds, omitting the misleading first since-boot
line. Recommended when you want only "current activity".
.TP
.B vmstat \-a 1
Use the active/inactive memory display rather than buffers/cache.
.TP
.B vmstat \-S M
Show memory columns in mebibytes (1024² bytes). \fB\-S m\fR uses
decimal megabytes.
.TP
.B vmstat \-d 1
Per-disk I/O activity, refreshed every second.
.TP
.B vmstat \-s
Memory and event-counter dump: roughly equivalent to a labelled view
of \fI/proc/meminfo\fR, \fI/proc/stat\fR and \fI/proc/vmstat\fR
relevant lines combined.
.TP
.B vmstat \-f
Total processes forked since boot, on a single line.
.TP
.B vmstat \-t 2
Append a wall-clock timestamp to each sample line. Useful when
piping output to a log.
